---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { GITHUB_REPO } from '../lib/catalogue';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, graph } from '../lib/schema';

const trail = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '' },
  { name: 'Add a template', path: 'contribute/' },
];

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Make a folder for the app',
    text: 'Fork the repository and add a folder under templates/. Its name is the template id: lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter. The id never changes once published.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Write its files',
    text: 'A template.yaml, a cloudgate.yaml, at most one docker-compose.yml, any config files under files/, and a square logo. Add an index.md for this website.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Check it as Cloudgate will read it',
    text: 'Run cloudgate template check on the folder. It runs the same check a Cloudgate install runs, with no install and no key, and says why a template would be hidden.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Open a pull request',
    text: 'CI runs the same check on every template. A pull request merges only when it passes, and the next release of the catalogue carries the new template to every install.',
  },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Which apps make a good template?',
    answer:
      'Open-source apps people run for themselves, with an official, versioned container image. A template should set up a working app on the first Apply, with nothing left to do but sign in.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why must every image name a version tag?',
    answer:
      'So that setting up a template today and next month gives the same app. A template using latest, or no tag, is refused by the check. Digests are not required.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a template write a fixed domain name?',
    answer:
      "No. A template names the compose service and port a Domain points at, never its host. On a Server each Domain starts on a generated host the user may change at setup; in a cloud account, the user types it.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Which config files can a template ship?',
    answer:
      'Text files its own containers read, listed in template.yaml under files and kept in the files/ folder: .conf, .cfg, .ini, .yaml, .yml, .json, .toml, .xml, .properties, .sql, .txt, .html, .css, .tmpl, or a Caddyfile. Scripts, programs, archives and hidden files are refused.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are index.md and screenshots part of the template?',
    answer:
      'No. They are this website’s files. The check skips them, and the catalogue a Cloudgate install downloads never holds them.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = graph(
  breadcrumbSchema(trail),
  {
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to add a template to the Cloudgate catalogue',
    step: steps.map((step, index) => ({
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      position: index + 1,
      name: step.name,
      text: step.text,
    })),
  },
  faqSchema(faq),
);
---

<Base
  title="Add a template"
  description="How to add an open-source app to the Cloudgate template catalogue: the folder layout, the files a template holds, and the check every template passes before it merges."
  path="contribute/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
    <header class="head">
      <h1>Add a template</h1>
      <p>
        A template is a folder with a few small files. If you can write a compose file for an app,
        you can add it to the catalogue every Cloudgate install reads.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="layout">
      <div class="main">
        <section aria-labelledby="steps-title" class="block">
          <h2 id="steps-title">From folder to catalogue</h2>
          <ol class="steps">
            {
              steps.map((step) => (
                <li>
                  <h3>{step.name}</h3>
                  <p>{step.text}</p>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="layout-title" class="block">
          <h2 id="layout-title">What goes in the folder</h2>
          <pre class="tree"><code>templates/ghost/
  template.yaml        <span>name, description, version, tags, links, values</span>
  cloudgate.yaml       <span>the Project config: each Service and its kind</span>
  docker-compose.yml   <span>only when the app has a compose part</span>
  files/               <span>config files its containers read</span>
  logo.svg             <span>square, at most 100 KB (or logo.png)</span>
  index.md             <span>this website's page for it</span>
  screenshots/         <span>optional, for this website</span></code></pre>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="yaml-title" class="block">
          <h2 id="yaml-title">template.yaml</h2>
          <p>
            What only a template has. Every field is checked strictly, so a misspelt field hides the
            template and is named, rather than being skipped.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`name: Ghost
description: A publishing platform for blogs and newsletters.
version: "5.96"
tags: [blog, cms]
links:
  website: https://ghost.org
  docs: https://ghost.org/docs
  source: https://github.com/TryGhost/Ghost
cloudgate: 0.1.0
compose: app
size: small
inputs:
  DB_PASSWORD: { generate: password }
  ADMIN_EMAIL:
    ask: { label: Admin email, format: email }
domains:
  - { service: ghost, port: 2368 }`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Each value the app needs has exactly one rule: <code>generate</code> it (a password, a
            hex, base64 or UUID key, or a signed JWT), <code>ask</code> the user for it, or fix its
            <code>value</code>. Values are made once, at setup, and saved like any typed value.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="index-title" class="block">
          <h2 id="index-title">index.md</h2>
          <p>
            The page this website shows for the template. Write what the app is, what setting it up
            creates, and what to do after the first Deployment, such as creating the admin account.
            Questions in its front matter appear on the page and in its structured data.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`---
faq:
  - question: How do I create the first admin account?
    answer: Open the Domain setup gave Ghost and add /ghost to the address.
---

## What you get

Ghost runs as a compose part beside its own PostgreSQL database...`}</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="check-title" class="block">
          <h2 id="check-title">Check it before you open a pull request</h2>
          <pre><code>npx @octabyte-io/cloudgate template check templates/ghost</code></pre>
          <p>
            It prints each template with a tick, or a cross and why Cloudgate would hide it, and makes
            no network request: the check is the CLI's own copy of Cloudgate's.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="block">
          <Faq items={faq} title="Questions about adding a template" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="side">
        <h2>Ready to start?</h2>
        <p>Read the repository's README, then fork it and add your folder.</p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href={`${GITHUB_REPO}/fork`}>Fork cg-templates</a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href={`${GITHUB_REPO}/issues/new`}>Request an app instead</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-block: 1.5rem 3rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-title);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .head p {
    color: var(--tx-2);
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
  }

  .layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 17rem;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 3vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .main {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 2rem + 2vw, 3.5rem);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .block {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .block > h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .block > p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--tx-2);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .block code {
    font-size: 0.875em;
  }

  pre {
    margin: 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 1rem 1.125rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-m);
    background: var(--panel);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  pre code {
    font-size: inherit;
  }

  .tree span {
    color: var(--tx-3);
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: step;
  }

  .steps li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 2.75rem;
    counter-increment: step;
  }

  .steps li::before {
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 1.875rem;
    height: 1.875rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--brand-line);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--brand-tx);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .steps h3 {
    padding-top: 0.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-body);
  }

  .steps p {
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    color: var(--tx-2);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .side {
    position: sticky;
    top: 5.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--brand-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-l);
    background: var(--brand-tint);
  }

  .side h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-sect);
  }

  .side p {
    color: var(--tx-2);
    font-size: var(--text-ui);
  }

  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .layout {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .side {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
